{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal to denominate the budget in bytes only fails to account for per-request overhead (e.g., TLS handshakes, connection setup), which can dominate cost in many environments. A byte budget allows an unbounded number of requests as long as total bytes remain under the cap, leading to denial-of-service or cost surprises that silently degrade the agent's resource usage rather than providing a hard, auditable terminal. The target acknowledges this as a countercondition but does not incorporate it into the budget mechanism, leaving the solution incomplete and vulnerable to the very silent degradation the problem seeks to prevent.","counterexample":["An agent repeatedly fetching tiny 1-byte resources over expensive TLS connections could exhaust a monetary budget while the byte counter shows minimal usage, causing a cost overrun that is not captured by the byte-only terminal."],"target":"50f4a6cbdc818a1016027bec3eb09027c6d709d677fb58ca76a299c67101d0de"},{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal relies on a cost model that may be learned, but does not specify how to ensure the model parameters are fixed and deterministic at the start of a run. If the model is learned from data that varies between runs, the budget becomes non-deterministic, breaking replayability. The target acknowledges this risk but offers no mechanism to guarantee parameter fixation, thus failing to provide a fully replayable typed terminal.","counterexample":["An agent that learns a cost model from previous runs; on replay, the learned model differs, causing the exhaustion point to shift, so the terminal is not auditable across replays."],"target":"ac5d06c42f1d7cfc16a3f21752637edf5437c0092fb6b5fd433e70657b09f70d"}]}